version 0.16.0
module adapter-front

# A small front panel: a 5 V adapter plugs into the jack, a capacitor takes the input ripple, and a
# green LED says the rail is there. The jack's switch contact goes out as its own port, because a
# switch contact that is wired to ground would short the input when no plug is in it.
#
# The two classes this case is about are stated here, not bought here: `dc-jack-th` says the plug
# size, the current and the voltage it has to take; `led-indicator-0603` says the colour and the
# bin the colour was measured at. Which parts fill them is the procurement layer's business
# (assets/procurement/primary/adapter-front.teda).
#
# The jack states `rated_current 2A` and the design says the same current runs through its tip, so
# the requirement on the pin and the requirement in the class agree (ADR-0026 D18). The jack has
# three contacts, so the stress check cannot pair its voltage requirement with two nets; the report
# says so instead of reading the jack as checked.
instances
  C1  IDa1b2c3  cap-mlcc-0402       100n   10%    16V  X7R
  D1  IDk7m2n4  led-indicator-0603  green  565nm
  J1  IDp3x8q4  dc-jack-th          2.0mm  2A     6V
  R1  IDt5w9r2  res-thick-0402      1k     ±1%

nets
  GND:    C1.2  D1.1  J1.2
  LED_A:  D1.2  R1.2
  PLUG:   J1.3
  VIN:    C1.1  J1.1  R1.1

ports
  GND   GND
  PLUG  PLUG
  VIN   VIN

constraints
  current  J1.1   2A
# The LED's anode is behind the series resistor, so it never rises above the rail it comes from.
# The window is what lets the resistor's dissipation be compared at all.
  voltage  LED_A  0V..6V
